{"skill":{"slug":"xdrop","displayName":"Xdrop"},"version":{"version":"1.0.0","createdAt":1774563632963,"changelog":"Initial release of xdrop skill.\n\n- Upload and download files or directories to/from an Xdrop server via CLI scripts.\n- Supports share-link workflows with encryption; downloads require full share link with `#k=...`.\n- Includes CLI flags: `--quiet`, `--json`, `--expires-in`, `--output`, `--api-url`, and others.\n- Provides usage examples, gotchas, and guardrails for safe, automated file transfers.\n- Requires Bun, network, and filesystem access.","changelogSource":"auto","license":"MIT-0","files":[{"path":"SKILL.md","size":3107,"sha256":"f96fbb1d010b464442df22414661d785ba9fda89664d57b2fbbec83f8503e8dc","contentType":null},{"path":"scripts/download.mjs","size":10758,"sha256":"0cd22c54fff41b48094b34ea1aa8eb4fcc48415db8f50483579d6ad9fa654e8a","contentType":"text/javascript"},{"path":"scripts/upload.mjs","size":20235,"sha256":"2900dccb6b69bf99f67cd62afc92ce9b1ac667cad9014b0af40ce3f62e37c5e4","contentType":"text/javascript"},{"path":"skill-card.md","size":1679,"sha256":"60faada372c6dc463d646f4abb17b34741751d90327476563e3ee432a7e50003","contentType":"text/markdown"}],"security":{"status":"suspicious","hasWarnings":true,"checkedAt":1789436749002,"model":null,"hasScanResult":true,"sha256hash":"6a49d25c24fec6c345a75167c5ec4411f19ce06da8291fce3315e855cfc7444e","virustotalUrl":"https://www.virustotal.com/gui/file/6a49d25c24fec6c345a75167c5ec4411f19ce06da8291fce3315e855cfc7444e","scanners":{"vt":{"status":"clean","verdict":null,"normalizedStatus":"clean","analysis":null,"source":"engines","checkedAt":1780086629942},"skillspector":{"status":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","score":43,"severity":"MEDIUM","recommendation":"CAUTION","issueCount":10,"checkedAt":1789436749002},"llm":{"status":"suspicious","verdict":"suspicious","normalizedStatus":"suspicious","confidence":"medium","summary":"The skill is a coherent Xdrop file-transfer helper, but its downloader trusts server-controlled URLs and filesystem paths enough to create review-worthy network and overwrite risks.","dimensions":[{"detail":"Local filesystem and network access are expected for this skill, but download.mjs fetches manifest and chunk URLs supplied by the server without scheme, host, private-network, or redirect constraints.","label":"Credentials","name":"environment_proportionality","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"No package install hook, dependency installation, autostart, or hidden setup mechanism was found in the inspected artifacts.","label":"Install Mechanism","name":"install_mechanism","rating":"ok"},{"detail":"The instructions are mostly user-directed, but they describe network access to the target Xdrop server without clearly warning that the server can supply additional manifest and chunk URLs fetched by the local process.","label":"Instruction Scope","name":"instruction_scope","rating":"note"},{"detail":"No persistent service or privilege escalation was found, but download.mjs writes decrypted files with truncating writes under a predictable or user-selected output directory and does not reject symlinks before writing.","label":"Persistence & Privilege","name":"persistence_privilege","rating":"concern"},{"detail":"Uploading chosen files and downloading/decrypting Xdrop links is consistent with the declared purpose, and the scripts implement local encryption/decryption rather than unrelated behavior.","label":"Purpose & Capability","name":"purpose_capability","rating":"note"}],"guidance":"Install only if you trust the Xdrop server or share links you will use, and choose fresh output directories that other local users or processes cannot pre-populate. This does not appear malicious, but the implementation should be reviewed before use in sensitive networks or shared workspaces.","findings":"[AE1] unexpected: SkillSpector's coverage concern was downgraded after manual inspection because the referenced upload and download scripts were present in the artifact and inspected directly.\n[T09] expected: The server-controlled manifest and chunk URL fetching is part of the file-transfer design, but the lack of URL validation creates a real blind-SSRF style risk when using an untrusted or compromised Xdrop server.\n[T09] expected: Writing downloaded files is expected, but following symlinks with truncating writes can let a local attacker who controls the output tree cause writes outside the intended destination.","agenticRiskFindings":null,"riskSummary":null,"model":null,"checkedAt":1789436749002}}}}}